# Apache HttpComponents HttpClient's fluent builder wires hostname
# verification through an X509HostnameVerifier handed to the SSL socket
# factory's constructor, and the verifier is optional everywhere else in the
# library: HttpClientBuilder exposes it only through setHostnameVerifier and
# leaves the field null until a caller sets it, while the 4.3
# SSLConnectionSocketFactory constructor it was handed to stored the argument
# verbatim and ran its verify path only when the field was non-null. A build()
# that threads this.hostnameVerifier straight into that constructor therefore
# wires a connection factory whose hostname verification is silently off for
# every caller that builds a client the ordinary way (CWE-20; the effect is
# CWE-295). GHSA-pqwh-44jj-p5rm/NVD state it directly: "does not ensure that
# X509HostnameVerifier is not null." The fix (08140864, svn trunk@1528614)
# reads the field into a local and defaults it to
# SSLConnectionSocketFactory.BROWSER_COMPATIBLE_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER when null,
# before any of the three constructor branches.
#
# Vocabulary. bindings.java.unguardedHostnameVerifierWiring reads three facts
# off the wiring function's body: the hand-off -- a hostnameVerifier argument
# reaching an SSLConnectionSocketFactory or the deprecated SSLSocketFactory
# constructor, read off the call-argument pair that flagScopeCallArgHit
# computes from argument flow, which is what keeps a same-named getter, a
# TestX509HostnameVerifier class reference or other bare mentions of the word
# from satisfying the clause; the null guard -- no hostnameVerifier == null /
# != null comparison in the function, the shape of the fix; and the
# provenance exclusions -- no hostname-verifier-named call (a constructed
# BrowserCompatHostnameVerifier, DefaultHostnameVerifier or any project's own
# *HostnameVerifier implementation is known non-null) and no hostnameVerifier
# parameter (a pass-through helper's null-ness is its caller's to settle).
# The concept is reused: code.CertValidationDisabled (VYQL-CFG-004) already
# carries the hostname-verification-off judgement for the explicit opt-outs
# (setHostnameVerifier(true), NoopHostnameVerifier); this binding reaches the
# same finding at the wiring site, so no concept or rule is minted.
#
# Discrimination, three --cache off scans of the whole checkout per revision,
# identical every run: 266 findings with exactly 1 VYQL-CFG-004 at
# HttpClientBuilder.java:692 (build()) on 08140864^, and 265 findings with 0
# VYQL-CFG-004 on 08140864 -- the fingerprint sets differ by exactly that one
# row (fp=7b4fe0963ae8964b), and the pre-binding baseline on the vulnerable
# revision is the same 265, so the binding adds the target and nothing else.
# The grep-enumerated
# reachable set -- every SSLConnectionSocketFactory/SSLSocketFactory
# construction in the tree and in the /tmp/cve-work clones -- holds no other
# site the binding reports: the internal static factories and ClientCustomSSL
# pass the BROWSER_COMPATIBLE/ALLOW_ALL constants (not the paired argument),
# TestSSLSocketFactory passes hostVerifier or a literal null, the documented
# ClientConfiguration example constructs its verifier in the same function,
# gradle's HttpClientConfigurer threads a parameter, hive constructs a
# DefaultHostnameVerifier, and the artemis guard spells the parameter's own
# null check. Measured on the clone trees: gradle resources-http 30 findings
# with 0 from this binding, artemis WebServerComponentTest unchanged (its two
# CFG-004 rows are the pre-existing NoopHostnameVerifier binding's), hive
# HiveConnection and SecurityUtils 0, undertow TestHttpClient 0. The needles
# are absent from BenchmarkPython; BenchmarkJava's one construction
# (Utils.getSSLFactory) passes NoopHostnameVerifier.INSTANCE, which the paired
# argument clause does not read, and its pre-existing CFG-004 row is
# unchanged.
#
# verify_current_expected: fixed, 1 -> 0 on the real revision pair.
test "rank1186: build() threads its possibly-null hostnameVerifier straight into the SSL socket factory"
  lang java
  file HttpClientBuilder.java
  expect VYQL-CFG-004
  expect_review code.CertValidationDisabled
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.http.impl.client;

  import org.apache.http.conn.HttpClientConnectionManager;
  import org.apache.http.conn.socket.LayeredConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLConnectionSocketFactory;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.SSLContexts;
  import org.apache.http.conn.ssl.X509HostnameVerifier;

  public class HttpClientBuilder {

      private HttpClientConnectionManager connManager;
      private LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory;
      private X509HostnameVerifier hostnameVerifier;
      private SSLContext sslcontext;
      private boolean systemProperties;

      public final HttpClientBuilder setHostnameVerifier(final X509HostnameVerifier hostnameVerifier) {
          this.hostnameVerifier = hostnameVerifier;
          return this;
      }

      public CloseableHttpClient build() {
          HttpClientConnectionManager connManager = this.connManager;
          if (connManager == null) {
              LayeredConnectionSocketFactory sslSocketFactory = this.sslSocketFactory;
              if (sslSocketFactory == null) {
                  if (sslcontext != null) {
                      sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(sslcontext, hostnameVerifier);
                  } else {
                      if (systemProperties) {
                          sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(
                                  (javax.net.ssl.SSLSocketFactory) javax.net.ssl.SSLSocketFactory.getDefault(),
                                  split(System.getProperty("https.protocols")),
                                  split(System.getProperty("https.cipherSuites")),
                                  hostnameVerifier);
                      } else {
                          sslSocketFactory = new SSLConnectionSocketFactory(
                                  SSLContexts.createDefault(),
                                  hostnameVerifier);
                      }
                  }
              }
          }
          return null;
      }
  }
  ```

